1
00:00:07,837 --> 00:00:17,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Audio notes on some excerpts from Anguttara Nikaya's book of fours, translated by Venerable Aryamangsa.

2
00:00:20,858 --> 00:00:32,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, endowed with four things, a foolish, unlearned, bad man keeps himself wounded, injured, and blameworthy, and subject to criticism by the wise, he accumulates much demerit.

3
00:00:33,966 --> 00:00:34,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Which four?

4
00:00:36,688 --> 00:00:42,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Without having found out, without having scrutinized, he speaks praise of one who should not be praised.

5
00:00:43,913 --> 00:00:50,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Without having found out, without having scrutinized, he speaks dispraise of one who should be praised.

6
00:00:51,132 --> 00:00:57,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Without having found out, without having scrutinized, he manifests clarity in the area which should not be clarified.

7
00:00:58,857 --> 00:01:05,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Without having found out, without having scrutinized, he manifests no clarity in an area which should be clarified.

8
00:01:06,703 --> 00:01:17,890
[SPEAKER_00]: These bhikkhu are the four things endowed with which foolish, unlearned, bad man keeps himself wounded, injured and is blameworthy and subject to criticism by the wise.

9
00:01:18,471 --> 00:01:20,312
[SPEAKER_00]: He accumulates much demerit.

10
00:01:22,605 --> 00:01:24,226
[SPEAKER_00]: This is pretty self-explanatory.

11
00:01:24,286 --> 00:01:34,710
[SPEAKER_00]: However, it's important to take this seriously, basically.

12
00:01:35,131 --> 00:01:50,137
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, the common view is, among those who try to practice the dhamma, but have not necessarily developed the right view yet, the common view is, well, as long as I'm trying my best,

13
00:01:53,471 --> 00:01:54,776
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not doing any harm.

14
00:01:56,302 --> 00:01:57,185
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's eh...

15
00:01:59,458 --> 00:02:00,979
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly what this sutta pointed.

16
00:02:00,999 --> 00:02:04,201
[SPEAKER_00]: There were some other suttas in the beginning of Anguttara Nikaya, if I remember correctly.

17
00:02:06,082 --> 00:02:15,947
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't matter whether you think that what you're praising, what you're sort of singling out as word in dhamma and so on.

18
00:02:16,928 --> 00:02:20,050
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter whether you genuinely believe it or not.

19
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:24,372
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is whether that thing actually is so or not.

20
00:02:24,492 --> 00:02:28,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it is according to the dhamma, whether it is according to the practice.

21
00:02:29,235 --> 00:02:32,596
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it says here that a foolish man hasn't scrutinized.

22
00:02:37,078 --> 00:02:41,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Hasn't scrutinized and he did not find out for himself.

23
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:53,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means it's a 50-50 chance that whatever that man is praising and finding praiseworthy, he might actually be blameworthy.

24
00:02:54,203 --> 00:02:56,264
[SPEAKER_00]: From the ultimate, obviously, point of view of the dhamma.

25
00:02:57,667 --> 00:03:04,691
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, what that man might be thinking it's important for the practice, it's important for the clarity, it might not be.

26
00:03:06,092 --> 00:03:11,775
[SPEAKER_00]: So, before that man starts praising and starts pushing for it and...

27
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:23,507
[SPEAKER_00]: starts sort of instructing others even, he should know it for himself, whether it is according to the dhamma, in which case, yeah, it is praiseworthy, whether it isn't.

28
00:03:24,128 --> 00:03:28,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Often, what people might be judging as blameworthy, it actually isn't.

29
00:03:29,602 --> 00:03:31,323
[SPEAKER_00]: There are numerous examples in the suttas.

30
00:03:32,064 --> 00:03:49,578
[SPEAKER_00]: There's even the one where, eh... well, it was in relation to the monks, but obviously works for anyone, ordained or non-ordained, where he said, oh, here is a man who is, eh... very agreeable to live with, very polite with others, very softly spoken, lovely person, everybody loves him.

31
00:03:50,679 --> 00:03:57,745
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh... he's completely full of wrong views, anatta-putujana, has no distinction, has no knowledge, has no attainments.

32
00:03:59,836 --> 00:04:03,059
[SPEAKER_00]: So, everybody will praise him, obviously, because he's such a nice person.

33
00:04:03,959 --> 00:04:10,505
[SPEAKER_00]: And nobody will find him blameworthy due to the fact that he's not even a sotāpanna.

34
00:04:10,605 --> 00:04:12,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, the Buddha says he should be blameworthy.

35
00:04:12,627 --> 00:04:13,468
[SPEAKER_00]: He should be blamed.

36
00:04:14,309 --> 00:04:15,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Because anybody can learn.

37
00:04:16,608 --> 00:04:19,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Nice, soft politeness and words and so on.

38
00:04:19,550 --> 00:04:22,111
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, it's not either or as well.

39
00:04:22,191 --> 00:04:24,112
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a person can be both.

40
00:04:24,492 --> 00:04:31,375
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in case that it isn't, what you should look for is the views, the way they live, the way they teach.

41
00:04:32,276 --> 00:04:38,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the way the society or the group would deem appropriate or not.

42
00:04:39,159 --> 00:04:46,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the Buddha goes on saying, eh... the... another type of man is... is hard to live with and they can't...

43
00:04:47,179 --> 00:04:53,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, people don't like them, but they are sotāpannas or arahants, fully enlightened.

44
00:04:54,923 --> 00:04:59,745
[SPEAKER_00]: But people blame them, because obviously they have no criteria for what is blameworthy.

45
00:04:59,765 --> 00:05:13,172
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what this sutta talks about in terms of the one who does do that, who is aware that he doesn't fundamentally have a criteria, but then goes ahead praising or blaming, he accumulates much demerit.

46
00:05:15,768 --> 00:05:16,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Carries on here.

47
00:05:16,689 --> 00:05:28,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Because endowed with four things are wise, learned, good man, keeps himself unwounded, uninjured, and is blameless, and not subject to criticism by the wise, and he accumulates much merit.

48
00:05:29,779 --> 00:05:30,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Which four?

49
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:35,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Having found out, having scrutinized, he speaks praise of one who should not be praised.

50
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:44,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Having... oh, yeah, and then there's another thing.

51
00:05:44,501 --> 00:05:45,742
[SPEAKER_00]: He speaks this praise.

52
00:05:47,623 --> 00:05:49,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often people think, oh, I must not criticize.

53
00:05:51,345 --> 00:05:55,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you should not criticize from your own vanity point of view or conceit.

54
00:05:56,248 --> 00:06:01,652
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're talking about a perspective of practice, in this case, there's clearly right and wrong.

55
00:06:02,412 --> 00:06:06,875
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't point out wrong, well, that's your fault.

56
00:06:09,738 --> 00:06:12,279
[SPEAKER_00]: He speaks this praise of one who should not be praised.

57
00:06:13,059 --> 00:06:17,921
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when I say you point out wrong, you don't want to like ranting on all bells and so on.

58
00:06:18,322 --> 00:06:29,226
[SPEAKER_00]: But if asked, if the context is there, the Buddha himself would list all the wrong ways people would be practicing at the same time, wrong things they're doing, misguided things they're doing.

59
00:06:29,806 --> 00:06:36,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often by investigating why the wrong thing is wrong, you get to understand what's right.

60
00:06:38,304 --> 00:06:44,130
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having found out, having scrutinized, he manifests no clarity in an area which should not be clarified.

61
00:06:44,671 --> 00:06:48,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Having found out, having scrutinized, he manifests clarity in an area which should be clarified.

62
00:06:48,916 --> 00:06:51,479
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much not knowing what's important.

63
00:06:52,547 --> 00:06:59,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing unimportant from important and then regarding unimportant things is that which you should really investigate and put loads of effort in.

64
00:06:59,774 --> 00:07:12,566
[SPEAKER_00]: And it often happens these days, where people confuse practice with sort of learning of a behavior, like just refining your politeness and behavior is confused for the practice of dhamma.

65
00:07:12,666 --> 00:07:14,028
[SPEAKER_00]: And loads of effort goes into it.

66
00:07:14,588 --> 00:07:15,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And as I said, it's not either or.

67
00:07:15,948 --> 00:07:16,568
[SPEAKER_00]: You can do both.

68
00:07:16,648 --> 00:07:24,090
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you do that, thinking that's what practice of dhamma is, well, then you won't be finding out what practice of dhamma is, because you already think you found it.

69
00:07:25,270 --> 00:07:27,570
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it will lead to much of your demerit.

70
00:07:28,851 --> 00:07:33,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Whoever praises the blameworth, or criticizes, he should be criticized.

71
00:07:33,792 --> 00:07:37,532
[SPEAKER_00]: He selects with his mouth a misdeed.

72
00:07:37,932 --> 00:07:41,193
[SPEAKER_00]: With this misdeed, he does not experience happiness.

73
00:07:43,080 --> 00:07:46,104
[SPEAKER_00]: That concludes that excerpt on the first page.

74
00:07:46,725 --> 00:07:53,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, we will include the PDF from which I'm reading this in the link in the description video, so people can download it for themselves.

75
00:07:55,038 --> 00:07:56,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, there's a change of topic now.

76
00:07:56,319 --> 00:08:16,433
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... bhikkhus, if, while walking, a thought of sense-desire, or a thought of ill-will, or a thought of harming arises for a bhikkhu, and if that bhikkhu tolerates it, does not abandon it, does not remove it, does not get rid of it, does not bring it to non-existence, then bhikkhus, while walking, that bhikkhu is said to have become thus.

77
00:08:16,873 --> 00:08:21,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Not ardent, unafraid of wrongdoing, constantly, continuously lazy, lacking energy.

78
00:08:23,559 --> 00:08:36,416
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the important distinction here is to notice that, whether it's a thought of sense-desire, ill-will, of harming, they arise on their own.

79
00:08:36,476 --> 00:08:38,879
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, if a thought arises for a bickle,

80
00:08:39,790 --> 00:08:46,395
[SPEAKER_00]: It might not seem like a big deal, but it's often very crucial, because people... people tend to overreach.

81
00:08:46,855 --> 00:08:59,745
[SPEAKER_00]: When people... when people sort of try to practice this attitude of not tolerating, not welcoming, getting rid of it, they more like end up acting out of aversion, of trying to get rid of the unpleasant feeling caused by that which they don't want.

82
00:09:00,726 --> 00:09:03,488
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's just another way of revolving around your own craving.

83
00:09:04,627 --> 00:09:08,848
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to recognize the extent of the arisen phenomena.

84
00:09:09,489 --> 00:09:12,610
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I say extent, I mean how far it has arisen on its own.

85
00:09:13,650 --> 00:09:17,871
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you don't add anything to it or you're trying to take away from it.

86
00:09:18,231 --> 00:09:22,973
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you know how far you should go and then not cross that mark.

87
00:09:24,205 --> 00:09:29,947
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says here, if a thought arises, or will, will, sense, desire, harming, if it arises for a bhikkhu...

88
00:09:30,588 --> 00:09:32,709
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that in itself, it's not a problem.

89
00:09:32,789 --> 00:09:34,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Arising of a thought, it's what mind does.

90
00:09:35,490 --> 00:09:36,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts arise for the mind.

91
00:09:38,231 --> 00:09:48,115
[SPEAKER_00]: But then the problem becomes, if that bhikkhu starts tolerating it, if he doesn't then make the effort to abandon, he doesn't remove it,

92
00:09:49,453 --> 00:10:02,000
[SPEAKER_00]: In a sense of... like, say, a thought of essential desire arisen, and he welcomed it, he automatically just welcomed it, and then he doesn't make any effort to abandon that welcoming, he doesn't make any effort to stop tolerating it.

93
00:10:03,741 --> 00:10:04,621
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the problem is.

94
00:10:04,961 --> 00:10:11,985
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, if a person starts discerning this... the threshold after which

95
00:10:13,905 --> 00:10:18,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Their effort should be applied, or before, depending how you look at it.

96
00:10:18,346 --> 00:10:26,768
[SPEAKER_00]: But if they are able to discern the extent of the phenomenon arising on its own, and then see what they are responsible for in it.

97
00:10:27,468 --> 00:10:29,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you are not responsible for its arising.

98
00:10:29,689 --> 00:10:39,751
[SPEAKER_00]: You're responsible for its maintenance, through tolerating, welcoming, indulging it, being careless, delighting in it.

99
00:10:41,841 --> 00:10:48,244
[SPEAKER_00]: But once that's discerned, then you realize you cannot unintentionally slip away from your mindfulness.

100
00:10:48,704 --> 00:10:52,266
[SPEAKER_00]: You slip away from your mindfulness when you intentionally choose to ignore this.

101
00:10:52,767 --> 00:10:59,150
[SPEAKER_00]: When you intentionally choose to ignore the arisen thought as something that you should not be welcoming and delighting in.

102
00:11:00,261 --> 00:11:02,402
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, as a result, you welcome it and you delight it.

103
00:11:02,522 --> 00:11:04,422
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how mindfulness is lost.

104
00:11:04,962 --> 00:11:07,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you stop watching a moment after a moment or you slipped.

105
00:11:08,663 --> 00:11:08,783
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

106
00:11:08,863 --> 00:11:12,184
[SPEAKER_00]: It's by giving in to unwholesome, even on the mental level.

107
00:11:13,885 --> 00:11:19,126
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's important to know what the extent of that phenomena is, as in the extent of its independence.

108
00:11:20,047 --> 00:11:22,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Because otherwise, you'll be denying everything and nothing.

109
00:11:22,427 --> 00:11:26,168
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be trying to fix everything, even when you don't even know whether it's a problem or not.

110
00:11:27,569 --> 00:11:28,549
[SPEAKER_00]: And that makes a problem.

111
00:11:30,222 --> 00:11:37,147
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, he goes on saying, if while standing, a thought of sense desire, ill will or harming arises for a bhikkhu and that bhikkhu tolerates, he does not abandon it.

112
00:11:38,168 --> 00:11:43,272
[SPEAKER_00]: If while sitting down, if while lying, if while walking.

113
00:11:43,372 --> 00:11:46,254
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter the way your body is disposed.

114
00:11:46,394 --> 00:11:54,661
[SPEAKER_00]: If thoughts have arisen on their own there, you have to be vigilant, not in regards to the content of your thoughts, but in regards to its nature, their nature.

115
00:11:55,461 --> 00:11:58,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Are those thoughts partaking in the domain of sensuality?

116
00:11:58,222 --> 00:12:00,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Are those thoughts partaking in the domain of ill-will?

117
00:12:00,924 --> 00:12:03,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Are those thoughts partaking in the domain of harming?

118
00:12:05,126 --> 00:12:06,707
[SPEAKER_00]: If so, you don't welcome it.

119
00:12:06,767 --> 00:12:15,151
[SPEAKER_00]: You let it endure there, because it has a reason on its own, but because of that, if you don't interfere, it will cease on its own.

120
00:12:15,191 --> 00:12:18,012
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the great insight of a Sotāpanna.

121
00:12:18,032 --> 00:12:22,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever has the nature of arising, because of that, it will have to cease.

122
00:12:24,238 --> 00:12:34,804
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's people's confusion and ignorance in regard to the manifestation of phenomena, that they get all caught up and bound by those phenomena, and affected.

123
00:12:36,725 --> 00:12:48,812
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if bhikkhus, while sitting down, a thought of sense desire, a thought of ill-will, or a thought of harm arises for a bhikkhu, and if that bhikkhu does not tolerate it, he abandons it, he brings it to non-existence,

124
00:12:49,858 --> 00:12:56,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it's said that he has become ardent, afraid of wrongdoing, constantly, continuously resolved and energetic, resolute.

125
00:12:57,144 --> 00:12:58,265
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, ye...

126
00:13:00,047 --> 00:13:06,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Resolved and energetic is a description of somebody who has recognized where the work is.

127
00:13:06,372 --> 00:13:15,380
[SPEAKER_00]: And the work is not in denying your thoughts, not in denying mind from thinking, not in denying thoughts from manifesting, but in denying your delight in it.

128
00:13:16,166 --> 00:13:20,950
[SPEAKER_00]: You're careless welcoming, you're careless non-abandoning, you're careless indolence.

129
00:13:21,991 --> 00:13:25,153
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just remove that bit, and you become resolved and energetic.

130
00:13:25,393 --> 00:13:28,116
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to be dealing with everything, all the time.

131
00:13:28,976 --> 00:13:36,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, if things are not sensual, not based on ill-will, not based on anger and harm, there is no problem.

132
00:13:36,843 --> 00:13:40,706
[SPEAKER_00]: You will automatically be mindful, because you're not engaging in anything unwholesome.

133
00:13:41,106 --> 00:13:41,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's, eh...

134
00:13:42,147 --> 00:13:44,529
[SPEAKER_00]: There was, by the way, there was, in Gūtara 4.11, eh...

135
00:13:47,830 --> 00:13:50,551
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, let's have a look at the next excerpt.

136
00:13:52,712 --> 00:13:54,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhikkhus, there are these four strivings.

137
00:13:55,013 --> 00:13:55,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Which four?

138
00:13:56,134 --> 00:14:02,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Striving by restraint, striving by abandoning, striving by developing, striving by guarding.

139
00:14:03,497 --> 00:14:05,478
[SPEAKER_00]: And which bhikkhus is striving by restraining?

140
00:14:06,838 --> 00:14:12,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu having seen a sight with the eye, is not one who grasps the signs.

141
00:14:12,901 --> 00:14:16,843
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not the one who grasps the attributes of that sight.

142
00:14:17,783 --> 00:14:24,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Having heard a sound with the ear, having smelled a smell with the nose, having tasted a taste with the tongue, having touched a touch with the body,

143
00:14:45,674 --> 00:14:51,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Having imagined an image with the mind, he's not the one who grasped the sign, he's not the one who grasped the attributes.

144
00:14:52,738 --> 00:14:55,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Since by dwelling with the mind-faculty unrestrained...

145
00:14:56,240 --> 00:15:01,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Since by dwelling with the mind-faculty unrestrained, even unwholesome phenomena of covetousness and grief could invade him.

146
00:15:02,423 --> 00:15:07,986
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is the way of... this is pretty much how the wisdom begins.

147
00:15:08,026 --> 00:15:10,768
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize things before,

148
00:15:11,948 --> 00:15:13,810
[SPEAKER_00]: They come your way when they affect you.

149
00:15:14,051 --> 00:15:15,532
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to start foreseeing it.

150
00:15:15,572 --> 00:15:18,055
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you foresee that...

151
00:15:18,075 --> 00:15:29,669
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not necessarily guarding yourself from this particular side or that particular side, although that's how it's going to end up being, because you're guarding yourself from the nature of unguarded I.

152
00:15:30,795 --> 00:15:35,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Having the experience of unguarded I can then result in those sights and sounds.

153
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:42,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't want to be just avoiding things that are unwholesome to look at, smell, taste, hear and touch and think about.

154
00:15:43,090 --> 00:15:46,774
[SPEAKER_00]: You actually want to see that the attitude of the mind that precedes

155
00:15:49,923 --> 00:15:51,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Engaging with those type of things.

156
00:15:52,165 --> 00:15:54,646
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you want to start restraining.

157
00:15:55,247 --> 00:16:01,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then, all you need to do is keep an eye on your eye, so to speak, and you don't... nothing can enter it.

158
00:16:02,632 --> 00:16:10,997
[SPEAKER_00]: But no matter of avoiding unwholesome things, if you don't keep an eye on your eye, you can only avoid those things only so far.

159
00:16:11,117 --> 00:16:15,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Sooner or later, something's going to slip through and your mind will be affected by covetousness and grief.

160
00:16:17,808 --> 00:16:18,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the next one is, eh...

161
00:16:18,969 --> 00:16:20,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Which bhikkhus is striving by abandoning?

162
00:16:21,010 --> 00:16:24,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, bhikkhus... A bhikkhu does not tolerate an arisen thought of sense-desire.

163
00:16:24,832 --> 00:16:25,553
[SPEAKER_00]: He abandons it.

164
00:16:25,613 --> 00:16:26,373
[SPEAKER_00]: He removes it.

165
00:16:28,775 --> 00:16:30,956
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what, eh... what we just spoke about.

166
00:16:31,596 --> 00:16:32,897
[SPEAKER_00]: He brings it to non-existence.

167
00:16:33,117 --> 00:16:34,438
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he stops fueling it.

168
00:16:35,259 --> 00:16:36,219
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it will subside.

169
00:16:37,540 --> 00:16:39,861
[SPEAKER_00]: He does not tolerate, eh... thought of ill-will.

170
00:16:42,463 --> 00:16:45,485
[SPEAKER_00]: And does not tolerate evil unwholesome phenomena whenever they arise.

171
00:16:46,749 --> 00:16:49,390
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever they arise, he stops tolerating their arising.

172
00:16:49,650 --> 00:16:50,790
[SPEAKER_00]: He stops welcoming it.

173
00:16:52,730 --> 00:16:54,151
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what means you get rid of it.

174
00:16:54,991 --> 00:16:59,032
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't prevent it from arising, but you can prevent it from being fueled further.

175
00:16:59,592 --> 00:17:00,752
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it will stop arising.

176
00:17:01,633 --> 00:17:05,934
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is no fuel for it, there will be no ground for its manifestation.

177
00:17:09,414 --> 00:17:09,974
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, eh...

178
00:17:10,054 --> 00:17:12,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Which bhikkhus is striving by developing?

179
00:17:15,636 --> 00:17:20,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Here Bhikkhu develops the awakening factor of mindfulness, which is dependent on seclusion.

180
00:17:20,904 --> 00:17:46,308
[SPEAKER_00]: dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in relinquishment, he develops the awakening factor of discrimination of phenomena, dhamma-vicā, he develops the awakening factor of energy, the awakening factor of joy, the awakening factor of tranquility, he develops the awakening factor of composure, develops the awakening factor of equanimity, or indifference, which is dependent on seclusion, dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in relinquishment.

181
00:17:47,395 --> 00:17:51,497
[SPEAKER_00]: That is called striving by developing.

182
00:17:52,297 --> 00:17:53,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Striving by guarding?

183
00:17:57,359 --> 00:17:58,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, okay, yeah, this is interesting.

184
00:17:59,240 --> 00:18:02,481
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in which bhikkhus is striving by guarding?

185
00:18:02,721 --> 00:18:10,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu guards the arisen helpful sign of composure, which is samādhi nimittam.

186
00:18:11,914 --> 00:18:21,765
[SPEAKER_00]: The perception of bones, the perception of worm-infested corpse, the perception of discolored corpse, the perception of decaying corpse, the perception of bloated corpse.

187
00:18:22,246 --> 00:18:23,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhikkhu is called striving by guarding.

188
00:18:24,028 --> 00:18:25,630
[SPEAKER_00]: These bhikkhus are the four strivings.

189
00:18:26,170 --> 00:18:31,376
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is already on the level of proper samādhi, the proper peripheral mindfulness.

190
00:18:32,468 --> 00:18:35,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is exactly where the sign of composure arises.

191
00:18:35,590 --> 00:18:39,313
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't attend to the sign of an arisen thing.

192
00:18:39,433 --> 00:18:47,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Signs and tokens of an arisen thing are its characteristics, its significance, that it's implied in it, which is inherently in the background.

193
00:18:47,458 --> 00:18:49,820
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the context of the thing you are attending to.

194
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:56,124
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start discerning the context, the sign of composure.

195
00:18:56,504 --> 00:19:00,827
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you start discerning that there is composure of your mind present.

196
00:19:02,484 --> 00:19:10,889
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to the general perception of the nature of bones, the nature of infested corpse, the discolored corpse...

197
00:19:11,689 --> 00:19:18,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, those are not things that you have to keep looking at or actively thinking about.

198
00:19:18,533 --> 00:19:21,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Those perceptions that have arisen

199
00:19:22,222 --> 00:19:48,821
[SPEAKER_00]: are relevant here, because if you discern them on that peripheral level as that which is in common to your living body, say, decaying corpse, decaying corpse or a living body, well, they all share the same matter, the same body, or rather the nature of the matter and the body, which means the decaying corpse and the living body pertain to the same nature.

200
00:19:49,402 --> 00:19:50,943
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your mind is composed

201
00:19:52,171 --> 00:19:58,275
[SPEAKER_00]: On a more fundamental perception of a decaying corpse, and it's more fundamental, it's because that's how things will inevitably end up.

202
00:20:01,418 --> 00:20:03,779
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize that the living...

203
00:20:04,740 --> 00:20:07,622
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that the decaying corpse is absence of life.

204
00:20:08,502 --> 00:20:12,165
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that the living body is a delayed decaying corpse.

205
00:20:13,772 --> 00:20:15,033
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to give priority.

206
00:20:16,394 --> 00:20:24,260
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to give the priority to that which brings dispassion, non-attachment and so on.

207
00:20:24,481 --> 00:20:27,183
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what... that's how these perceptions are helpful.

208
00:20:28,625 --> 00:20:31,888
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like you have to keep thinking about it, bringing it forward, bringing it forward.

209
00:20:31,928 --> 00:20:32,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

210
00:20:32,168 --> 00:20:33,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that won't reveal the nature of it.

211
00:20:34,490 --> 00:20:43,798
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to think about a discolored corpse or decaying corpse, and then you want to see, discern its nature being relevant and applicable to you.

212
00:20:44,839 --> 00:20:54,947
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how your mind is composed upon that phenomena, which is... which does not contain greed, infatuation, intoxication with life, and so on.

213
00:20:55,976 --> 00:21:03,259
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, protecting that type of composure is what's called the four right strivings, the striving by guarding.

214
00:21:05,320 --> 00:21:07,641
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it finishes in verses.

215
00:21:17,357 --> 00:21:18,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Here is ardent.

216
00:21:19,079 --> 00:21:21,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, with these, bhikkhu here is ardent.

217
00:21:22,283 --> 00:21:24,206
[SPEAKER_00]: He has reached the destruction of suffering.

218
00:21:24,886 --> 00:21:25,868
[SPEAKER_00]: That's saṅgutra 4.14.

